1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,702
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,722 --> 00:00:08,434
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:08,834 --> 00:00:10,829
This is Lecture 21,

4
00:00:10,849 --> 00:00:18,254
control prototypes of the pattern sewer and the flat-knitting machine.

5
00:00:18,654 --> 00:00:20,414
Two prototypes today.

6
00:00:20,434 --> 00:00:25,415
For the pattern sewer we work from the window to the motor;

7
00:00:25,435 --> 00:00:31,180
for the knitting machine, from the lead to the carriage's top speed.

8
00:00:32,700 --> 00:00:39,104
A shirt factory sews thousands of pocket flaps a day and asks the lab for a

9
00:00:39,124 --> 00:00:41,160
pattern-sewer prototype.

10
00:00:41,560 --> 00:00:50,349
It uses a standard lockstitch head and an X–Y table on 10 mm lead ball screws,

11
00:00:50,369 --> 00:00:57,578
with a 400 W servo per axis: 1.27 newton-metres rated, 3.8 peak.

12
00:00:57,978 --> 00:01:05,998
The shop wants 3 mm stitches at 1600 r/min.

13
00:01:06,398 --> 00:01:13,457
The template may move only during the 183.4 degrees when the needle is out of

14
00:01:13,477 --> 00:01:14,376
the cloth.

15
00:01:14,776 --> 00:01:18,360
Is the motor big enough, and how fast can it sew?

16
00:01:18,760 --> 00:01:19,963
We'll answer at the end.

17
00:01:21,483 --> 00:01:24,583
Pattern sewer: from window to motor.

18
00:01:26,103 --> 00:01:27,875
The chain starts from the window.

19
00:01:28,275 --> 00:01:36,132
With 1.5 millimetres of fabric and template, and 10 degrees kept at each end,

20
00:01:36,152 --> 00:01:39,712
the usable window is 183.4 degrees.

21
00:01:40,112 --> 00:01:46,819
At n r/min each degree takes one over 6n seconds,

22
00:01:46,839 --> 00:01:52,859
so at 1600 there are only 19.1 milliseconds.

23
00:01:53,259 --> 00:01:59,830
The firmware uses an S-curve so the table isn't excited;

24
00:01:59,850 --> 00:02:05,479
the price is a peak factor of 5.33 instead of 4.

25
00:02:05,879 --> 00:02:13,266
A 3 mm stitch then needs 43.8 metres per second squared,

26
00:02:13,286 --> 00:02:22,657
a peak speed of 0.314 metres per second, about 1884 r/min at the motor,

27
00:02:22,677 --> 00:02:25,699
well below rated speed.

28
00:02:27,219 --> 00:02:32,079
The torque goes almost entirely into the rotor and the screw.

29
00:02:32,479 --> 00:02:39,089
The four inertias add to about 0.664 times ten to the minus four;

30
00:02:39,109 --> 00:02:45,108
times the angular acceleration, plus a small friction term,

31
00:02:45,128 --> 00:02:51,432
the X axis needs about 1.84 newton-metres peak, far below 3.8.

32
00:02:51,832 --> 00:02:54,480
But will it overheat in continuous sewing?

33
00:02:54,880 --> 00:02:56,613
Look at rms torque.

34
00:02:57,013 --> 00:03:00,717
The S-curve form factor is 0.816,

35
00:03:00,737 --> 00:03:07,148
and in the worst case every stitch uses the whole window:

36
00:03:07,168 --> 00:03:14,934
together 0.58 times the peak, which must stay within the 1.27 rating.

37
00:03:15,334 --> 00:03:18,954
So the peak may only reach 2.18.

38
00:03:19,354 --> 00:03:26,241
Working back gives the design accelerations: 50 for X, 40 for Y.

39
00:03:27,761 --> 00:03:33,181
Turn the chain around and every stitch length gets a speed limit.

40
00:03:33,581 --> 00:03:39,852
The time to move s is the square root of C times s over a;

41
00:03:39,872 --> 00:03:45,168
the window over six times that time is the limit,

42
00:03:45,188 --> 00:03:48,639
capped by the head's 2500 r/min.

43
00:03:49,039 --> 00:03:58,139
A 3 mm stitch along X can run at 1709, meeting 1600.

44
00:03:58,539 --> 00:04:08,396
Along Y the mass is bigger and the design acceleration only 40, so 1528.

45
00:04:08,796 --> 00:04:19,216
At 45 degrees each axis moves s over root two, and it reaches 1818.

46
00:04:19,616 --> 00:04:24,880
This table goes into the firmware for automatic slow-down,

47
00:04:24,900 --> 00:04:30,254
and becomes the expected values of Chapter 22's test cases.

48
00:04:31,774 --> 00:04:35,834
Pattern-sewer firmware: interpolating by angle.

49
00:04:37,354 --> 00:04:40,874
Each stitch has its own cap,

50
00:04:40,894 --> 00:04:48,334
but the spindle can't jump from 2092 to 1080 in one stitch.

51
00:04:48,734 --> 00:04:50,914
Look-ahead runs two passes.

52
00:04:51,314 --> 00:04:58,920
Backward: no stitch may be more than 300 r/min faster than the next,

53
00:04:58,940 --> 00:05:04,303
so a slow stitch ahead brakes the spindle early.

54
00:05:04,703 --> 00:05:10,882
Forward: no stitch may be more than 300 faster than the one before,

55
00:05:10,902 --> 00:05:13,843
so acceleration isn't too harsh.

56
00:05:14,243 --> 00:05:23,080
In the chart, a run of 2 mm stitches has one 6 mm stitch along Y.

57
00:05:23,480 --> 00:05:30,702
Braking starts four stitches early, reaches 1080 right at the long stitch,

58
00:05:30,722 --> 00:05:34,420
then climbs back one stitch at a time.

59
00:05:34,820 --> 00:05:41,400
This is computed once per pattern segment, in the background.

60
00:05:42,920 --> 00:05:46,820
Scaled knitting machine: needle-by-needle timing.

61
00:05:48,340 --> 00:05:53,294
Industrial flat-knitting machines have hundreds of needles,

62
00:05:53,314 --> 00:05:56,160
two or three systems and two beds.

63
00:05:56,560 --> 00:06:03,856
The teaching prototype shrinks it to the minimum: one system, one bed,

64
00:06:03,876 --> 00:06:06,051
60 needles, gauge E5.

65
00:06:06,451 --> 00:06:09,988
The coarse gauge means big needle slots,

66
00:06:10,008 --> 00:06:16,301
cams and selector jacks that an ordinary mill or laser cutter can make,

67
00:06:16,321 --> 00:06:18,791
and generous selection time.

68
00:06:19,191 --> 00:06:26,078
The carriage is driven by a 200 watt servo through a belt,

69
00:06:26,098 --> 00:06:29,531
at 0.01 millimetre per count.

70
00:06:29,931 --> 00:06:36,506
The selector has 8 segments, so neighbouring needles go to different segments;

71
00:06:36,526 --> 00:06:39,381
one segment fires every 8 needles,

72
00:06:39,401 --> 00:06:43,525
67.7 milliseconds apart at 0.6 metres per second,

73
00:06:43,545 --> 00:06:48,260
far more than the 3 milliseconds to pull in and release.

74
00:06:49,780 --> 00:07:00,438
This is the needle bed and carriage of Lab 21-1, slowed twenty times.

75
00:07:00,838 --> 00:07:06,680
On top, the carriage speed: accelerate, cruise, decelerate,

76
00:07:06,700 --> 00:07:10,258
with a pause at each end to reverse.

77
00:07:10,658 --> 00:07:16,361
Below, the red line is the selection point; as the carriage passes each needle,

78
00:07:16,381 --> 00:07:19,042
the selector must decide right there.

79
00:07:19,442 --> 00:07:25,088
The time base is no longer spindle angle but carriage position:

80
00:07:25,108 --> 00:07:30,843
needle i should be selected at c plus, i plus one half, pitches.

81
00:07:31,243 --> 00:07:42,787
One row is 464.8 millimetres and takes about 0.935 seconds.

82
00:07:44,307 --> 00:07:47,967
The knitting firmware changes only one thing:

83
00:07:47,987 --> 00:07:53,283
the time base becomes carriage position instead of spindle angle.

84
00:07:53,683 --> 00:07:58,682
The selector takes Tr from command to armature motion,

85
00:07:58,702 --> 00:08:02,120
so the command goes v times Tr early.

86
00:08:02,520 --> 00:08:05,240
There's one more term, half of Tq:

87
00:08:05,260 --> 00:08:08,945
an MCU checks position once per control cycle,

88
00:08:08,965 --> 00:08:14,504
so after passing the command point it still waits for the next cycle,

89
00:08:14,524 --> 00:08:16,840
half a cycle late on average.

90
00:08:17,240 --> 00:08:22,741
An FPGA compares at every count, so that term almost vanishes.

91
00:08:23,141 --> 00:08:26,144
A lead can cancel a fixed delay;

92
00:08:26,164 --> 00:08:33,135
what it can't cancel is the quantisation of plus or minus half a cycle and

93
00:08:33,155 --> 00:08:36,441
the scatter J of the response time.

94
00:08:37,961 --> 00:08:46,486
Run the standard numbers: 0.6 metres per second, Tr 1.5 milliseconds,

95
00:08:46,506 --> 00:08:49,706
a 1 millisecond MCU cycle.

96
00:08:50,106 --> 00:08:56,882
The lead is 1.20 millimetres, about 0.24 of a pitch; uncompensated,

97
00:08:56,902 --> 00:09:01,446
we'd already be at the edge of mis-selection.

98
00:09:01,846 --> 00:09:06,582
After compensation two terms remain:

99
00:09:06,602 --> 00:09:16,358
quantisation 0.30 millimetres and scatter 0.18, together 0.48 millimetres,

100
00:09:16,378 --> 00:09:19,396
about 0.094 of a pitch.

101
00:09:19,796 --> 00:09:26,040
Two criteria: within a quarter pitch the jack is still pushed correctly;

102
00:09:26,060 --> 00:09:31,521
acceptance is stricter, a tenth of a pitch, a 2.5 times margin.

103
00:09:33,041 --> 00:09:39,490
Set the worst error equal to a tenth of a pitch and you get the speed limit:

104
00:09:39,510 --> 00:09:43,660
0.635 metres per second with a 1 millisecond MCU;

105
00:09:43,680 --> 00:09:48,682
with a 0.25 millisecond cycle the formula gives about 1.20;

106
00:09:48,702 --> 00:09:51,917
with an FPGA comparing at every count,

107
00:09:51,937 --> 00:09:58,981
quantisation shrinks to half a count and the selector's own scatter sets the limit,

108
00:09:59,001 --> 00:09:59,918
about 1.68.

109
00:10:00,318 --> 00:10:04,167
Counter-intuitively, a smaller Tr doesn't help,

110
00:10:04,187 --> 00:10:07,131
because the lead already cancels it;

111
00:10:07,151 --> 00:10:11,906
the scatter J and the control period are what limit speed.

112
00:10:12,306 --> 00:10:16,088
So the E5 prototype starts with an MCU,

113
00:10:16,108 --> 00:10:20,279
and an FPGA is the first step to go faster.

114
00:10:21,799 --> 00:10:24,936
When a robot picks parts from a conveyor,

115
00:10:24,956 --> 00:10:30,788
the camera and the gripper are triggered by the conveyor encoder's position,

116
00:10:30,808 --> 00:10:35,794
not by time, exactly like selecting needles by carriage position.

117
00:10:36,194 --> 00:10:42,247
The controller adds a lead of speed times the response time plus half a

118
00:10:42,267 --> 00:10:43,444
polling cycle,

119
00:10:43,464 --> 00:10:46,609
the same formula as needle selection.

120
00:10:47,009 --> 00:10:53,526
And the lead can't cancel jitter: at 0.6 metres per second,

121
00:10:53,546 --> 00:11:02,832
a valve scatter of plus or minus 0.3 milliseconds is plus or minus 0.18 millimetres.

122
00:11:03,232 --> 00:11:09,497
That's why high-speed picking robots use the motion card's hardware

123
00:11:09,517 --> 00:11:11,748
position-compare output,

124
00:11:11,768 --> 00:11:16,532
the equivalent of an FPGA comparing at every count.

125
00:11:18,052 --> 00:11:24,232
In everyday life the closest thing to needle selection is a basketball pass.

126
00:11:24,632 --> 00:11:27,510
You throw ahead of a running teammate;

127
00:11:27,530 --> 00:11:32,161
the lead is roughly their speed times the ball's flight time,

128
00:11:32,181 --> 00:11:34,372
just like the selection lead.

129
00:11:34,772 --> 00:11:38,562
But your release is always a little early or late,

130
00:11:38,582 --> 00:11:43,592
and the faster your teammate runs, the further off the ball lands.

131
00:11:43,992 --> 00:11:49,315
That's exactly the scatter J that limits the carriage speed.

132
00:11:50,835 --> 00:11:53,295
Back to our problem.

133
00:11:53,695 --> 00:12:00,849
At 1600 r/min the template has 19.1 milliseconds per stitch;

134
00:12:00,869 --> 00:12:06,947
a 3 mm stitch needs 43.8 metres per second squared,

135
00:12:06,967 --> 00:12:14,600
and the X axis peaks at about 1.84 newton-metres, far below 3.8.

136
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:16,420
The motor is big enough.

137
00:12:16,820 --> 00:12:21,865
But continuous sewing is limited by rms torque,

138
00:12:21,885 --> 00:12:28,225
so the design accelerations are only 50 for X and 40 for Y.

139
00:12:28,625 --> 00:12:38,358
A 3 mm stitch along X can then run at 1709, meeting 1600; along Y only 1528,

140
00:12:38,378 --> 00:12:46,699
and the firmware's look-ahead slows those stitches automatically.

141
00:12:48,219 --> 00:12:52,370
Now Virtual lab 21-1, the scaled knitting prototype,

142
00:12:52,390 --> 00:12:56,380
which you can do yourself on WenQuest. The checks,

143
00:12:56,400 --> 00:12:59,669
a few readouts and the tasks are blurred;

144
00:12:59,689 --> 00:13:02,797
they're for you to work out in the lab.

145
00:13:02,817 --> 00:13:06,085
Top left are the needle bed and carriage,

146
00:13:06,105 --> 00:13:10,095
in the middle the selection error of every needle,

147
00:13:10,115 --> 00:13:12,582
and below a zoom on one needle.

148
00:13:12,982 --> 00:13:18,979
Press Knit one course first. The carriage accelerates, cruises and slows,

149
00:13:18,999 --> 00:13:25,162
the selection point decides needle by needle, and the error chart fills in.

150
00:13:25,562 --> 00:13:28,370
Task one: with standard settings,

151
00:13:28,390 --> 00:13:34,885
raise the top speed from 0.6 and watch when the error band breaks out of the

152
00:13:34,905 --> 00:13:36,770
green acceptance band.

153
00:13:36,790 --> 00:13:43,542
What's the highest speed that still passes? Estimate it from the formula first,

154
00:13:43,562 --> 00:13:45,942
then pin it down in the lab.

155
00:13:46,342 --> 00:13:50,929
Task two: press Switch to FPGA, firing at every count,

156
00:13:50,949 --> 00:13:55,878
and raise the speed again. Quantisation almost disappears.

157
00:13:55,898 --> 00:14:01,850
What sets the limit now, and how many more courses an hour do you get?

158
00:14:01,870 --> 00:14:03,642
Compare for yourself.

159
00:14:04,042 --> 00:14:06,414
Press Run 1000 courses,

160
00:14:06,434 --> 00:14:13,070
and count the mis-selected needles and those outside acceptance.

161
00:14:13,090 --> 00:14:15,982
Keep the two criteria apart.

162
00:14:16,382 --> 00:14:21,152
Task three: change to the finer E7 bed, still with the FPGA,

163
00:14:21,172 --> 00:14:25,143
at 1 metre per second, and switch to a fixed lead,

164
00:14:25,163 --> 00:14:28,975
then drag the fixed lead to find its best value.

165
00:14:28,995 --> 00:14:32,168
A fixed lead is right at only one speed,

166
00:14:32,188 --> 00:14:35,282
so needles on the ramps go wrong first.

167
00:14:35,682 --> 00:14:42,020
Then adjust the reversal margin so the carriage reaches cruising speed before

168
00:14:42,040 --> 00:14:43,424
the first needle,

169
00:14:43,444 --> 00:14:49,782
until all 60 needles pass. The guide and report template are on the platform.

170
00:14:51,302 --> 00:14:56,295
Everything in this lecture is on the WenQuest learning platform.

171
00:14:56,695 --> 00:15:03,120
You can open today's interactive slides and the knitting prototype and work

172
00:15:03,140 --> 00:15:04,323
them yourself,

173
00:15:04,343 --> 00:15:07,675
including the pages this video skipped.

174
00:15:08,075 --> 00:15:13,049
You submit the assignment and the lab report on the platform,

175
00:15:13,069 --> 00:15:15,669
and get marks and comments back.

176
00:15:16,069 --> 00:15:20,819
Questions go to the course discussion forum,

177
00:15:20,839 --> 00:15:27,865
and the grades page shows your mark on every assignment and quiz.

178
00:15:29,385 --> 00:15:32,802
Your assignment has four questions. One:

179
00:15:32,822 --> 00:15:36,927
fabric plus template is now 3 millimetres thick,

180
00:15:36,947 --> 00:15:43,200
so the geometric window becomes 192.6 degrees, still with 10 at each end.

181
00:15:43,220 --> 00:15:49,816
Find the speed limits for 2, 3 and 4 millimetre stitches along X and along Y,

182
00:15:49,836 --> 00:15:54,628
decide whether 3 millimetres along X still reaches 1600,

183
00:15:54,648 --> 00:15:58,925
and explain how look-ahead handles the Y stitches.

184
00:15:59,325 --> 00:16:05,754
Two: the 12 kilogram Y axis should also run at 50 metres per second squared.

185
00:16:05,774 --> 00:16:09,488
Find the peak and the worst-case rms torque,

186
00:16:09,508 --> 00:16:13,052
decide whether the 400 watt motor will do,

187
00:16:13,072 --> 00:16:17,125
and give at least two remedies with their costs.

188
00:16:17,525 --> 00:16:21,737
Three: the knitter becomes E6, a 4.23 millimetre pitch.

189
00:16:21,757 --> 00:16:25,815
Find the top carriage speed with a 1 millisecond MCU,

190
00:16:25,835 --> 00:16:30,815
a 0.5 millisecond MCU and an FPGA, check them in the virtual lab,

191
00:16:30,835 --> 00:16:36,432
and choose one. Four: with a 20 metres per second squared emergency stop,

192
00:16:36,452 --> 00:16:41,587
find the braking distance from 0.6, 1.0 and 1.66 metres per second,

193
00:16:41,607 --> 00:16:44,587
and where the limit switches should go.

194
00:16:44,987 --> 00:16:48,047
Submit the report on the WenQuest platform.

195
00:16:49,567 --> 00:16:53,497
There's also a ten-question quiz. For example,

196
00:16:53,517 --> 00:16:59,336
with a usable window of 183.4 degrees and the spindle at 2000 r/min,

197
00:16:59,356 --> 00:17:04,489
how many milliseconds does the template have for one stitch?

198
00:17:04,889 --> 00:17:06,799
Or, on a knitted sample,

199
00:17:06,819 --> 00:17:13,474
the needle numbers that go wrong always leave the same remainder when divided by 8.

200
00:17:13,494 --> 00:17:15,806
What's the most likely cause?

201
00:17:16,206 --> 00:17:21,699
You get three attempts, and the explanations appear as soon as you submit.

202
00:17:23,219 --> 00:17:24,719
Three things to remember.

203
00:17:25,119 --> 00:17:29,142
One: both prototypes share one control problem,

204
00:17:29,162 --> 00:17:32,841
actions landing in windows set by position,

205
00:17:32,861 --> 00:17:36,540
so budget the timing before choosing parts.

206
00:17:36,940 --> 00:17:42,019
Two: for the pattern sewer the window gives time and acceleration,

207
00:17:42,039 --> 00:17:46,731
inertia gives peak torque, heat sets the design acceleration,

208
00:17:46,751 --> 00:17:52,447
and look-ahead and angle-based interpolation keep the template still while

209
00:17:52,467 --> 00:17:53,760
the needle is in.

210
00:17:54,160 --> 00:17:59,111
Three: the knitting machine's lead cancels delay,

211
00:17:59,131 --> 00:18:06,820
and its top speed is set by the scatter J and the control period, not by Tr.

